    <section style="margin: 2.5rem 0;" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
      <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Mistakes in Age Calculation</h2>
      <ul style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li><strong>Assuming All Months Have 30 Days:</strong> Months vary between 28, 29, 30, and 31 days. Simple division by 30 produces inaccurate fractional day counts.</li>
        <li><strong>Overlooking Daylight Saving Time Shifting:</strong> Doing math with local wall-clock midnight can add or subtract an hour during spring and fall daylight transitions. Our calculator operates on UTC midnight timestamps to maintain absolute daily precision.</li>
        <li><strong>Neglecting Leap Day Rules:</strong> A person born February 29, 2000 does not have a February 29th in 2001, 2002, or 2003. Depending on local law, their legal birthday falls on February 28 or March 1.</li>
      </ul>
